WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270
Film Courage: What makes a great actor?

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:12.710
Robin Riker, Actor/Author: I think a great
actor is someone who can feel as they listen.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:20.000
Because I think the best moments happen when
you’re not talking.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:28.980
Your response to another person is something
that can feed them in that just having your

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.100
line when it’s your turn to speak doesn’t
you know.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.350
I can see it happen.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:42.970
I’ve been on both sides of the giving and
receiving of that where you have your moment

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:51.590
and what I do…just what my response is,
what my eyes say, what my body says…I can

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:58.230
see the other person going deeper into what
it is that they want to say because they know

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.110
that they are being heard.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:05.870
And it is a natural kind of symbiotic thing.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:11.610
A wonderful quote from either Julie Harris
(a fantastic actress) or Helen Hayes (another

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:18.070
fantastic actress), she was doing a play in
Broadway and she had to do it night after

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.920
night after night for months and months and
months.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.280
And there is a scene in which she comes downstage
and sits on the park bench and has a moment

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.950
and weeps.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:34.690
And they’re talking about the scene and
what she’s thinking and all that and the

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:40.200
person says to her (the interview says) “How
can you cry every night?”

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.350
And she says “How can you not?”

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.560
And that was the end of the quote…because
she was in that moment.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.990
She was feeling that feeling.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:02:00.420
She was hearing those words or feeling that
feeling and how can you not respond to that

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.740
if you’re really hearing?

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:14.110
So that’s what I think makes a wonderful
actor is being able to hear and feel whether

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.640
you are talking or not.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:23.880
Film Courage: Being so dialed in where you
are able to hear something with a fresh perspective

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:30.910
night after night to take you to that place,
but then developing the tough skin to not

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:34.510
let other things that are in the business
you down?

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.700
This sounds like an incredible balance?

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.860
Robin: Ahhh!

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.950
Therein is the rub!

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.850
Yes, it is not an easy thing we do, you know?

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:52.500
It’s challenging on so many levels because
so many other people you have a job, you have

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.590
a job, you go to that job, you do that job.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:02.210
This is one of the reasons why the economic
downturn affected me not at all because actors

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.270
are always looking for jobs so it’s not
“Oh my Gosh!

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.459
I lost my job.”

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:09.970
Because we’re always going from one to another.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:14.380
But you do have to balance that.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:21.280
You have to protect yourself from the slings
and arrows of this silly business, of this

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:31.349
capricious business in order to protect your
softy creamy center for when you actually

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:32.349
need it.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:33.240
Film Courage: Great analogy.

